ant's data is completely inaccessible to another.\n\nThe prompt details explicit 404 behavior for unauthorized access. This seems like a reasonable approach. If a user, even if signed in, tries to access a resource that doesn't belong to their tenant, they get a 404. This is good: a refusal is also an admission. It does not look as though we can determine whether or not the content does not exist.\n\nThe `/users` endpoint remains unchanged and needs to name *everyone* on the install. That is unusual. This is important to note and can be tricky. This endpoint potentially needs to bypass the tenant filter, or it could present information with the tenant clearly indicated in the data.\n\nInside a tenant everything works as expected. Sharing, page histories, and the visibility of published books for signed-out visitors should not be affected by the changes. This is standard multi-tenancy and is an important part of the acceptance criteria.\n\nFinally, the fixtures are the crux of the issue. The prompt is crystal clear: \"Our fixtures name no tenant, so rows without one behave as they do today, and `bin/rails test` stays green; don't edit it.\" I need to fully understand this implication. It is critical.\n\nThe phrase \"don't edit it\" could refer to both the fixtures *and* the tests, or just the existing fixtures. It is better not to edit them to keep existing tests valid. We may add new test files or tests that create tenant records in the test code, and inspect if `test/test_helper.rb` needs anything, but leave existing fixtures alone. This suggests that existing tests rely on the current state of the database and adding tenants to fixtures might break those tests. I will need to be extremely careful to ensure that the migration and new code do not negatively impact the existing codebase and tests.\n\n\n",
