on tests**:\n\n*   We'll start with book creation via `POST /books`. Confirm the book lands in the current user's tenant, and if `everyone_access: true` is set, only users *within* that tenant get access. `GET /books/new` and `GET /books/:id/edit` should only list users in the current tenant.\n*   Customer-A user accessing Customer-B resources: This is where we break out the big guns. Whether they are a member, administrator, or have a pre-existing \"Access\" row, if it's a Customer-B resource, it's a 404. Specifically:\n    *   Library (`GET /`): Customer-B books must *not* appear.\n    *   Book show (`GET /:id/:slug`): 404. User stays signed in.\n    *   Book edit/update/destroy (`GET /books/:id/edit`, `PATCH /books/:id`, `DELETE /books/:id`): 404.\n    *   Book publication: (`GET/PATCH /books/:id/publication`, `GET /books/:id/publication/edit`): 404.\n    *   Book bookmark (`GET /books/:id/bookmark`): 404.\n    *   Book search (`POST /books/:id/search`): 404.\n    *   Book leaf moves (`POST /books/:id/leaves/moves`): 404.\n    *   Leafable show (pages, sections, etc.): 404.\n    *   Leafable new/create/edit/update/destroy: 404.\n    *   Page edit history: 404.\n*   After the 404, the user's session remains intact, and subsequent requests to Customer-A resources should work.\n*   Verify `/users` lists all users across *all* tenants.\n*   Within a tenant (e.g., `tenant_a`): Own books, shared books, `everyone_access`, published books, and editing/history should all function normally.\n*   Ensure that an admin within a tenant *can* edit books in their own tenant.\n*   For signed-out visitors:\n    *   The root path (`/`) should list all published books across *all* tenants. No unpublished books.\n    *   `GET book_slug_path` and `GET leafable_slug_path\n\n",
